139: ## 3. THE DISAGREEMENT, BOTH SIDES IN THEIR OWN WORDS
140: 
141: **Q5 -- should the RUN itself go red when its self-check has failed and its forced failure is gone?**
142: 
143: * **Yes (Opus, measured):** "The run's own verdict is false to anything reading the trailer or the exit status ...
144:   Smallest change: make PASS depend on the flag only the G arm sets ... Byte-identity cost: none."  kimi-k3: "not
145:   adequate to the file's own contract ... any consumer of the file's own verdict (human, trailer-grep battery) is
146:   misled."  glm: "The G row alone leaves the run's own verdict a lie outside gate 2."
147: * **No (grok, panel):** "Leave the run's trailer alone ... Putting another `failures++` on the success path would turn
148:   every good run red and would break the byte-identity the measurement just established.  One sentence in the block,
149:   stating that this double edit is caught by gate 2's G row, is the record."  agy: "Yes [G is adequate] ... That is
150:   precisely why the G check was created."
151: * **My disposition: ADOPT the flag.**  grok's objection is to a `failures++` on the success path, which the flag is
152:   not: `sc_g` is 0 only when the G line did not print, so a healthy run is unchanged -- measured byte-identical on seven
153:   device variants at -O2 and -O0.  **grok and agy: is there a case where `sc_g` changes a healthy run, or a reason the
154:   run's verdict should stay PASS when its self-check failed?**
