1399:               "$(grep -c -F 'zero: ' "$RTCLOG")" "2"
1400:     #  *** THE SOLE DETECTOR OF THE CLAMP TARGET, and the first nine rows did not have it.
1401:     #  ***  Two panel seats said independently that no row asserts WHAT the clamp produces --
1402:     #  only that it is nonzero, positive, and did not remove the timer.  MEASURED rather than
1403:     #  predicted: `d->hz = (int) TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY;` changed to `d->hz = 1;` passed 9 of 9.
1404:     #  Deleting these three rows restores that hole, so they are named here.
1405:     check     "RTC range: the clamp-exactness rows are present" \
1406:               "$(grep -c -F 'clamped to exactly TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY' "$RTCLOG")" "3"
1407:     #  *** THE THRESHOLD WAS PINNED ONLY FROM ABOVE, AND THAT WAS A MEASURED FALSE PASS.
1408:     #  ***  Until this row the table sampled ONE in-range value (1000), so the bound could
1409:     #  be moved anywhere in [1000, INT_MAX] undetected.  The measure seat found the cheapest
1410:     #  form: TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY -> TIMER_MAX_CATCHUP, ONE IDENTIFIER, and the two macros sit
1411:     #  two lines apart in timer.h -- 12 of 12 green while every guest rate above 2^20 was
1412:     #  boosted to INT_MAX, which timer.h:72-77 itself calls unserviceable.
1413:     check     "RTC range: the threshold is pinned from BELOW" \
1414:               "$(grep -c -F 'above CATCHUP: passed through, NOT clamped' "$RTCLOG")" "1"
1415:     check     "RTC range: the boundary value itself is tested" \
1416:               "$(grep -c -F 'INT_MAX exactly: passed through, not clamped' "$RTCLOG")" "1"
1417:     #  What the TIMER CORE is told, as opposed to what the device stored.  Deleting
1418:     #  timer_update_frequency() passed 12 of 12: the detector captured last_timer_hz from the
1419:     #  start and no row ever read it.
1420:     check     "RTC range: the timer core's own input is asserted" \
1421:               "$(grep -c -F 'timer core got' "$RTCLOG")" "2"
1422:     #  THE CONSTBLIND GUARD.  The three clamp rows READ TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY, so they are
1423:     #  green for any value of it; this row asserts the ceiling IS INT_MAX, derived from
1424:     #  outside timer.h.  Named because deleting it silently restores the blindness -- and
1425:     #  because five seats voted to DROP the constblind row as "already fixed" while THIS
1426:     #  file, added after that audit, was a live instance of it.
1427:     check     "RTC range: the constant-blindness guard is present" \
1428:               "$(grep -c -F 'ceiling IS INT_MAX' "$RTCLOG")" "1"
1429: fi
1430: 
1431: # ---- #405: the ATA IDENTIFY capacity bytes --------------------------------------
1432: # Third instance of the same construction (float_emul, dev_sh4, now dev_wdc): the driver
1433: # stubs the diskimage_* externals and #includes dev_wdc.c, so the function that runs is
1434: # the one that ships. wdc_initialize_identify_struct() is static, so there is no way to
1435: # link it without including the file.
1436: #
1437: # *** WHY THE NAMED ROWS BELOW ARE THE WHOLE POINT. `% 255` and `& 255` return the SAME
1438: # byte for every operand below 255, so a table of plausible-looking disk sizes passes on
1439: # the UNFIXED code -- every disk under 33,423,360 bytes agrees. Each named row puts a
1440: # specific byte at or past the divergence point. Measured against the shipped defect:
1441: # the full revert fails 7 rows, and a PARTIAL fix that corrects only the two `>> 8`
1442: # lines still fails 4 -- which is the case a less careful table would have let through.
1443: # ***
1444: WDCBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_wdc_identify
1445: WDCLOG=$LOGDIR/diff_wdc_identify.log
1446: if ! $CC -O2 -std=c99 -I"$SEC/src/include" -I"$SEC/src/include/thirdparty" \
1447:         -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Wl,--gc-sections \
1448:         -o "$WDCBIN" "$HERE/diff_wdc_identify.c" > "$WDCLOG" 2>&1; then
1449:     note "wdc IDENTIFY differential compile failed:"; sed 's/^/       /' "$WDCLOG" | head -12
1450:     check "wdc IDENTIFY: compiles against the real dev_wdc.c" "no" "yes"
1451: else
1452:     check "wdc IDENTIFY: compiles against the real dev_wdc.c" "yes" "yes"
1453:     "$WDCBIN" > "$WDCLOG" 2>&1
1454:     sed 's/^/       /' "$WDCLOG"
1455:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: row failures" \
1456:               "$(grep -oE '[0-9]+ failures' "$WDCLOG" | grep -oE '^[0-9]+')" "0"
1457:     check_min "wdc IDENTIFY: rows actually run" \
1458:               "$(grep -oE '^[0-9]+ rows' "$WDCLOG" | grep -oE '^[0-9]+')" 42
1459:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: offline verdict" \
1460:               "$(grep -c 'WDC_IDENTIFY_PASS' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1461:     #  The four divergence rows. Deleting any one of them returns the table to the
1462:     #  vacuous state described above, so each is named rather than merely counted.
1463:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the >>8 threshold row is present" \
1464:               "$(grep -c 'threshold: >>8 byte reaches 255' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1465:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the carry row is present" \
1466:               "$(grep -c 'carry: >>8 wraps into >>16' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1467:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the >>16 row is present" \
1468:               "$(grep -c '>>8 accidentally right, >>16 wrong' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1469:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the >>24 threshold row is present" \
1470:               "$(grep -c 'threshold: >>24 byte reaches 255' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1471:     #  The spec-free oracle. There is no ATA document in this tree, so the absolute
1472:     #  encoding cannot be cited -- but diskimage_recalc_size() rounds every image up to
1473:     #  a whole cylinder, so the block must agree with its OWN geometry words. That
1474:     #  needs no specification at all, and it is what makes the fix defensible here.
1475:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the self-consistency oracle is present" \
1476:               "$(grep -c 'IDENTIFY agrees with itself' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1477:     #  Word 53 is 0x0002 -- asymmetric, so a +0/+1 packing swap is visible. Word 47 is
1478:     #  0x8080, a byte-swap palindrome, and would have been useless here.
1479:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the packing anchor is present" \
1480:               "$(grep -c 'packing anchor' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1481: 
1482:     #  #484: THE RING-OCCUPANCY ROWS.  Each is NAMED rather than counted, because
1483:     #  deleting any one returns the table to a state a measured mutant survives --
1484:     #  the same reason the four divergence rows above are named.
1485:     #
1486:     #  *** THE TWO ANCHORED ROWS ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE WITH THE REST. ***  Two
1487:     #  mutants that keep occupancy SOUND while destroying DATA -- the `return` moved
1488:     #  inside the warning latch, and drop-oldest -- are GREEN under every
1489:     #  occupancy-shaped and DRQ-shaped row here.  Only the marker byte at the tail
1490:     #  cell and the real consumer's FIFO order kill them.  An oracle computed the
1491:     #  same way as the code under test cannot see an edit that preserves the
1492:     #  computation -- clause (f) one level down.
1493:     #
1494:     #  MEASURED: 9 of these red on the pre-fix device and 0 after, with the two R6
1495:     #  liveness rows GREEN ON BOTH -- which is what makes the nine reds evidence
1496:     #  rather than a ring that never worked at all.  Kill table and logs in
1497:     #  regress/artifacts/wdcringambig/killtable_20260827/.
1498:     #
1499:     #  *** -cF, NOT -c, AND THE GATE CAUGHT ME OMITTING IT. ***  The label
1500:     #  `R2 anchor: inbuf[tail] survives` contains a BRACKET PAIR, which basic grep
1501:     #  reads as a CHARACTER CLASS -- `inbuf[tail]` matches inbuft / inbufa / inbufi /
1502:     #  inbufl and never the literal text.  It returned 0 and the gate went red, which
1503:     #  is the LOUD direction and the safe one.  Had the class happened to match
1504:     #  something, the row would have read as pinned while pinning nothing.
1505:     #
1506:     #  Fixed-string matching for every label in this block: a row NAME is literal
1507:     #  text, never a pattern.  Same class as this tree's existing `grep -nF` rule.
1508:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the ring liveness control" \
1509:               "$(grep -cF 'R6 liveness: SIZE-1 all land' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1510:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the wrap, in the file's own idiom" \
1511:               "$(grep -cF 'R1 full ring: pointer occupancy' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1512:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the guest-visible DRQ bit" \
1513:               "$(grep -cF 'R3 full ring: DRQ still set' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1514:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: ANCHORED -- the tail marker byte" \
1515:               "$(grep -cF 'R2 anchor: inbuf[tail] survives' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1516:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: ANCHORED -- the consumer's own order" \
1517:               "$(grep -cF 'R4 fifo: first pop is first push' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1518:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the disk-image sink" \
1519:               "$(grep -cF 'R5b sink: no MIS-SEQUENCED sector reaches the image' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1520:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the BULK arm (defensive; see the fix comment)" \
1521:               "$(grep -cF 'R7 memcpy arm: the anchor survives' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1522:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: pins the -1 in the bulk bound" \
1523:               "$(grep -cF 'R8 bulk edge: head never reaches tail' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1524: 
1525:     #  *** R9 IS THE ACCEPT SIDE, AND IT IS A SOLE CARRIER.  THREE SEATS NAMED THE
1526:     #  SAME ONE-CHARACTER ESCAPE INDEPENDENTLY ON PASS 2. ***  Tightening the bulk
1527:     #  bound `>` to `>=` refuses a refill that exactly fits, silently shortening the
1528:     #  transfer -- and it escaped ALL 119 rows, because R8 pins only the REFUSAL side.
1529:     #  MEASURED with R9 present: 122 rows, exactly 1 failure, and it is this row.
1530:     #
1531:     #  A guard is half-tested by showing it refuses what it should; the other half is
1532:     #  showing it ACCEPTS WHAT IT MUST, which is the half a bound-tightening edit
1533:     #  escapes.  Same reasoning as the de-escalation clause's accept rows, reached
1534:     #  from the opposite direction.
1535:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: ACCEPT side -- an exactly-fitting refill lands" \
1536:               "$(grep -cF 'R9 exact fit: the refill is ACCEPTED' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1537: 
1538:     #  *** R10 AND R11 CLOSE AN L2 FLOOR BREACH FOUND ON PASS 2, and all three
1539:     #  escapes had ONE cause: no row had free >= 512 with to_read >= 2, so the
1540:     #  MULTIPLIER `* to_read` was unpinned; and no row called wdc__read() TWICE, so
1541:     #  the bulk arm's latch interaction and its common tail were unpinned. ***
1542:     #
1543:     #    M3  `512 * to_read >` -> `512 >`   two tokens, 0 reds of 122, 275 integrity
1544:     #                                       violations -- clause (d), a bound RELAXED
1545:     #    M2  break moved inside the latch   0 reds; the SECOND read falls through and
1546:     #                                       head lands ON tail -- the ambiguity back
1547:     #    M1  break -> return                0 reds; the completion irq is dropped
1548:     #
1549:     #  Each now dies to exactly ONE row, and to a DIFFERENT one.  R11's first cut
1550:     #  filled the ring to free == 0 and the mutant SURVIVED -- the fall-through then
1551:     #  takes the byte-wise arm, whose per-byte guard saves it.  A mutant that will not
1552:     #  die is a RESULT: that one was the row mis-cut, not the target dead.
1553:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the multiplier is pinned (two sectors, one sector of room)" \
1554:               "$(grep -cF 'R10 multiplier: two sectors into one sector of room is REFUSED' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1555:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: a SECOND refused read keeps the ring unambiguous" \
1556:               "$(grep -cF 'R11 second refusal: head has NOT landed on tail' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1557:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: a SECOND refused read still reaches the common tail" \
1558:               "$(grep -cF 'R11 second refusal: the completion irq still reaches the tail' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1559: 
1560:     #  *** #485: ONE ROW, FOUR LEGS, AND FOUR OF SEVEN MUTANTS PASS LEG 1 ENTIRELY. ***
1561:     #  The round first shipped a two-observable sweep (ABRT, assert-count) over 256
1562:     #  opcodes.  A seat built five edits that escaped it, and each surviving leg below
1563:     #  exists because of one of them:
1564:     #
1565:     #    M9  delete dev_wdc_access's TAIL TICK      the repro called wdc_command() and
1566:     #                                               ran the tick BY HAND, so it was blind;
1567:     #                                               leg 1 drives the real register path
1568:     #    M4  hoist ONE assert after the switch      reads ABRT+NO-irq 0 and passes a
1569:     #                                               count oracle, while asserting at
1570:     #                                               DISPATCH for the three DEFERRED
1571:     #                                               opcodes -- a spurious completion irq
1572:     #                                               on every disk write a guest issues.
1573:     #                                               The silent set is pinned as an
1574:     #                                               IDENTITY {0x30,0xa0,0xc5}, not a count
1575:     #    M5  assert moved INSIDE the unimpl latch   only the FIRST unknown command per
1576:     #                                               device interrupts.  A sweep with one
1577:     #                                               fresh device per opcode CANNOT see it
1578:     #    M7  STATUS read stops clearing the field   int_assert is a LEVEL; the tick
1579:     #                                               re-raises it forever
1580:     #    M8  SET_FEATURES asserts only on abort     the sweep varies the command byte and
1581:     #                                               never `precomp`, so its SUCCESS path
1582:     #                                               never ran.  Input coverage in ONE
1583:     #                                               dimension is not input coverage
1584:     #
1585:     #  Measured: all seven mutants die, and M4/M5/M7/M8 read ABRT+NO-irq 0 -- they pass
1586:     #  leg 1 and die only to the legs those escapes forced.  Pre-fix the row reads
1587:     #  ABRT+NO-irq 242, silent 245, 2nd 0.
1588:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: a terminated command raises its irq" \
1589:               "$(grep -cF 'a terminated command raises its irq' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1590: 
1591:     #  #480: THE THREE CALL-SITE ROWS ARE NAMED, BECAUSE THE COUNT PIN IS NOT ENOUGH.
1592:     #
1593:     #  This round first DECLINED to name them, reasoning that the detector's own identity
1594:     #  row (`if (rows != 85)`) already reds the gate on any deletion, so naming would guard
1595:     #  a shut door -- which the ADDITION LICENCE refuses.  TWO SEATS REFUTED THAT BY
1596:     #  DIFFERENT ROUTES, and both refutations were then MEASURED:
1597:     #
1598:     #    D1_renumber      delete the call AND renumber the pin in the same edit
1599:     #                     -> 82 rows, 0 failures, WDC_IDENTIFY_PASS
1600:     #    D2_rowsplusplus  replace `row_rejected_cmd_resets();` with `rows ++;`
1601:     #                     -> 83 rows, 0 failures, WDC_IDENTIFY_PASS  <- no renumber at all
1602:     #
1603:     #  So the count pin binds the row COUNT and not row IDENTITY, and the SOLE CARRIER for
1604:     #  M4_afterguard could have been removed with this gate staying green.  These three
1605:     #  checks close exactly that mode.
1606:     #
1607:     #  WHAT THEY DO NOT CLOSE, stated because a guard whose scope is overclaimed is worse
1608:     #  than none: an oracle WEAKENED while keeping its label.  `rows` stays right, the
1609:     #  label still prints, and these greps still pass.  That remains kill-table territory.
1610:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the same-opcode reissue row is present" \
1611:               "$(grep -c 'a same-opcode reissue resets the ring' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1612:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the rejected-command row is present" \
1613:               "$(grep -c 'a rejected command still resets' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1614:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the refill-command row is present" \
1615:               "$(grep -c 'a refill command starts from empty' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1616: 
1617:     #  #481: the ALT-CTRL rows, named for the reason #480 MEASURED rather than assumed.
1618:     #
1619:     #  The detector's identity row binds the row COUNT, never row IDENTITY: replacing a
1620:     #  row's call with `rows ++` keeps the count right, the verdict green and this gate
1621:     #  passing while a sole carrier vanishes.  Two seats found that independently on #480
1622:     #  and it was negative-controlled.
1623:     #
1624:     #  THIS ROUND HAS TWO SOLE CARRIERS and they are the ones that matter most:
1625:     #    Md_nohelper (guard kept, abort removed)  -> only `an SRST aborts what was in
1626:     #                                                flight` reds
1627:     #    Md_widemask (mask widened to include IDS) -> only `an nIEN write is not a
1628:     #                                                reset` reds
1629:     #
1630:     #  Same honest scope as #480's block: naming closes DELETION and REPLACEMENT.  It
1631:     #  does NOT close an oracle weakened while keeping its label -- that stays kill-table
1632:     #  territory, and saying so is what keeps this guard from being overclaimed.
1633:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the SRST-is-a-reset row is present" \
1634:               "$(grep -c 'a real SRST (0x06) is a reset' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1635:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the routine-ctl row is present" \
1636:               "$(grep -c 'a routine 0x08 preserves the command' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1637:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the nIEN row is present" \
1638:               "$(grep -c 'an nIEN write is not a reset' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1639:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the SRST-abort row is present" \
1640:               "$(grep -c 'an SRST aborts what was in flight' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1641:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the SRST-signature accept row is present" \
1642:               "$(grep -c 'a real SRST still signs the CD-ROM' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
