22455: them.  The fix is the one `seat_verdicts.py` already has: key on the notice's position, never on the phrase alone,
22456: with two negative controls -- an answer that quotes the phrase stays answered; a real mid-answer wall still reds.
22457: **A second instance, the opposite direction:** on every panel from `panel_20260925_160838`, codex's 831-byte file -- the
22458: CLI banner, the prompt, and two `ERROR: You've hit your usage limit` lines -- is flagged `WALLED MID-ANSWER` in its
22459: detail line and then counted among the seats that "produced output" in the SEATS summary, which names only kimi as
22460: unanswered.  A reader of the summary line sees codex as answered.  Same fix: the summary counts what the detail line
22461: classified, never the byte count.
22462: 
22463: ### `wdcidcomparators` (MED, detector) — 104 of diff_wdc_identify.c's 128 rows compare where the self-check does not reach
22464: 
22465: `#551` gave `regress/diff_wdc_identify.c` a self-check on its two comparators, `chk()` and a new device-independent `row_cmp()`, and
22466: the file's LAST self-check pins its comparator call counts (`chk()` 24, `row_cmp()` 8, `#551f`).  Only 24 rows compare
22467: INSIDE a comparator the self-check feeds (16 `chk()` got/want calls and 8 `row_cmp()` calls); 8 more hand `chk()` a 0/1
22468: verdict computed at the call (derive: `grep -cE '\? [01] : [01], 1\);' regress/diff_wdc_identify.c`), so a verdict
22469: computation that goes inert is invisible to the self-check; the other 96 compare INLINE.  Owner ruling 2026-09-25: "24/128
22470: now, file the rest".  The route (the Fable 5.1 ruling on `#551`, item 5): move the inline and verdict rows onto
22471: comparators the self-check feeds, with the measured requirements `#551` met as the bar -- the original row output kept,
22472: adding only the SELFCHECK lines and the covered-failure report lines (four on `w57hi`, five on `w58lo`), at -O2 and
22473: -O0, across the seven `dev_wdc.c` variants; every vacuity control red.  (This said "byte-identical stdout except the
22474: SELFCHECK lines", which `#551` did not meet: its report lines are measured additions, `measured/run551f.out` section
22475: 2 -- GPT-6 Astra, confirming `#551`.)  Also owed here,
22476: disclosed by `#551` and measured (`wdcidselfcheck_20260925/measured/run551v2.out`, variant `rows`): a rows- or name-keyed
22477: test inside either comparator, placed before its `rows ++`, keeps every gate-2 row green while silencing any covered row
22478: except the self-mutant's.
22479: 
22480: ### `fbpendingselfcheckG` (LOW, harness) — two COVERED stems' self-checks are built weaker than `#551`'s: one cannot red gate 2's G row, the other runs first only
22481: 
22482: (1) `regress/diff_fbpending_bound.c` prints its SELFCHECK verdict through `row()`, the helper under test (`row("SELFCHECK
22483: the comparator can still fail", sc_bad == 0, ...)`), so its FAIL line still carries gate 2's G text and the G row cannot
22484: red there -- the Opus seat's reading on `#551`'s pass 1.  (2) `regress/diff_rtc_range.c`'s sentinel runs only FIRST,
22485: before any row -- the stateful window `#551` closed for `wdc_identify` by running first AND last -- glm's companion on the
22486: same pass; its failure branch omits G's text, so RTC's G row CAN red, and its problem is placement only (this heading
22487: said both stems' self-checks "cannot red gate 2's G row" -- false for RTC: GPT-6 Astra, confirming `#551`).  One filing, "sentinel placement and the G text across the COVERED stems".  `#551`'s block is the shape to
22488: copy: the G text printed ONCE, by the last run, only when both runs passed.  Each fix owes the control that an inert
22489: comparator reds G.
22490: 
22491: ### `wdcidlane` (MED, harness) — the gate-side close of `#551`'s contract boundary: a lane that fails a covered row, and the log read through a pipe
22492: 
22493: `#551`'s contract says what the file's own checks can see: every one reads state the file writes or output it prints,
22494: so an edit that reproduces a healthy run's observables is invisible to all of them -- its (C), measured twice on
22495: `#551f`'s pass: covered ring rows skipped with their labels and counts printed (green), and M19, an exit handler that
22496: seeks a file-redirected stdout to 0 and writes a healthy log over the real one -- which passes gate 2's file-redirected
22497: MAIN checks on dropold, drqring and w57hi (`wdcidselfcheck_20260925/opus551_measured_f2/analyze_f2.out`) but is
22498: rejected on all three by the existing self-mutant lane, which captures its log through a pipe (`reallane_f2.out`), so
22499: it does NOT keep the complete gate green (this said "green", reading the main checks as the gate, and cited the
22500: rescore, which covered `drqring` only -- GPT-6 Astra, confirming `#551`).  Two gate-side changes cross that
22501: boundary, and each is a gate change owing its own negative controls:
22502: 1. **a lane on device mutants that fail a covered row -- ONE capacity row through `row_cmp()` AND ONE ring row through
22503:    `chk()`, each on its own mutant**, because a skip of one group leaves the other group's lane green (the panel grok
22504:    on `#551f`'s pass, M16); a lane closes only the rows its mutants fail;
22505: 2. **gate 2 reads the log through a pipe** (`"$WDCBIN" 2>&1 | cat > "$WDCLOG"`), which no exit handler can seek: M19
22506:    red on the main checks on all three defects, pristine logs byte-identical file-vs-pipe, the self-mutant lane
22507:    unchanged -- it closes M19's route to the MAIN checks, which the lane already closes for itself
22508:    (`opus551_measured_f2/analyze_f2.out`, a fallback-model run whose logs are artifacts; the main loop re-scored
22509:    `drqring` and two M18 cells: `measured/rescore_m19.out`).
22510: The Fable 5.1 ruling on `#551f`'s pass closed the chain on wording and named these as the boundary's close.
22511: 
22512: ### `wdcrowpin` (LOW, harness) — gate 2 pins `diff_wdc_identify.c`'s row count only as `>= 42`
22513: 
22514: The file's own identity row (128) is the only exact ROW pin.  **As found on `#551d`'s pass**, deleting it AND an unnamed
22515: covered row was green: a composite -- the identity row's deletion alone is harmless, a covered row's alone is red.
22516: Raised by deepseek (DO NOT SHIP) and ruled a real pre-existing composite, filed rather than a floor breach of `#551`
22517: (`pass551d/fable5_pass551d.md`, G3).  **`#551e`'s call-count pin has since closed it for the COVERED rows**: deleting
22518: the identity row and one comparator call leaves the LAST self-check's count (`chk()` 24, `row_cmp()` 8) unsatisfied --
22519: a reading, corroborated by the measured pin/no-pin deletion pair (`measured/run551f.out`: red 127/1 with the pin,
22520: GREEN 127/0 without).  **What remains is the 96 INLINE rows**, which no counter sees: deleting the identity row and
22521: one inline row is still green, by reading.  (This entry said the covered-row composite IS green -- stale since
22522: `#551e`: GPT-6 Astra, confirming `#551`.)  Fix: a gate-side exact row pin, with its controls.
22523: 
22524: ### `wdcverdictone` (LOW, harness) — gate 2 accepts a wdc log that carries BOTH verdict tokens
22525: 
22526: `grep -c WDC_IDENTIFY_PASS` is pinned to 1, the self-mutant lane needs FAIL present, and nothing requires FAIL ABSENT on
22527: the main run (grok and kimi-k3 on `#551e`'s pass, H4).  Since `#551f` a covered failure's own report line reds the
22528: failure-count check whatever the trailer prints, so this is defence in depth for the inline rows, which `#551` does not
22529: cover.  Fix: the log carries exactly one of the two tokens -- cheap, a gate change with its own controls.
