HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:74:was identical to it: table5/CELLMAP.out) -- except the read of selector 0, whose edit is EQUIVALENT (NOT CLAIMED
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:91:      read of selector 0 cannot tell a skipped load from a real one (odata starts at 0: NOT CLAIMED (4)); COMMAND 0
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:98:      COMMAND value (NOT CLAIMED (8))
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:106:NOT CLAIMED (disclosed).  (0) An edit conditioned on TWO axes at once -- a joint cell such as "refuse 9 only by sb
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:111:bits are open at latch-clear (4-7, 9-31, 33-63): measured 117/117 at bits 4 and 40
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:176:is verified against the stored-int oracle only: a refusal that WRITES 5 reads as a keep (NOT CLAIMED (3)).  B depends
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:204:#  The refused value, captured whole: "0x8" can match only 0x8, never 0x80 (the digits run to " is").
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:226:#  Pinned independently of the helper: a wrong register field would otherwise pass silently.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:410:# ---------------------------------------------------------------- sessions
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:420:#  A: selector p written at a rotating width and read back through PORT into s<k> (poisoned 0xdead first, so a
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:421:#  read that never ran cannot pass -- selector 0 would otherwise match a register's reset value); DATA value 0x11*p
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:422:#  for p >= 1, read back through DATA INTO THE SELECTOR REGISTER, which holds p before the read, never 0x11*p.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:423:#  Port 0 is COMMAND: written with NOP (0, from zr) only.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:437:#  the DATA read-backs, one per port 1..7: re-select p, read DATA into the selector register
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:444:#  P: previous selector 5, then nine refusals with read-backs (the FIRST is 8), two count-only refusals, and the
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:445:#  twelfth after PORT=7.  The count is sampled after the twelfth, so a latch re-armed every N refusals, N <= 11, reds.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:450:#  16 by sb and by sh, each read back into a register whose own value (0x200000000, 0x100000003) is spent by then and
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:451:#  can never read as 5 -- so an ignored read, or a session that died, reds the row too.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:467:#  R: between two refusals, a PORT read, a DATA write, a NOP command, a DATA READ and a GET_RESOLUTION -- every kind of
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:468:#  traffic a guest makes except SET_RESOLUTION -- in its own session.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:482:#  F: the free-run executes the refused sw AND a guest lw of PORT into a poisoned t2 (a GPR nothing loads would
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:483:#  print the zero cpu_new() gave it on every device -- the channel has to be a guest load).
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:487:#  V: X1 across a refusal made while COMMAND still holds GET_RESOLUTION, then every cell across four refusals.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:488:#  Port 0 is never written after the first GET_RESOLUTION: a DATA write there EXECUTES the command.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:507:#  W: the width x boundary cells, each after its own non-zero predecessor into a poisoned read-back; then an ld.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:531:#  Y: COMMAND holds 1.  SET_RESOLUTION with X1 = Y1 = 0 (power-on) is declined by dev_fb_resize() with a message,
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:532:#  so nothing is resized; it runs twice, the second time between the two refusals.  The second refused value,
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:533:#  0x100000003, is 3 as an int -- never the kept 5.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:544:#  Z: later refusals at selectors 0-4 (the X sessions and F refuse only once, so nothing else holds silence there).
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:552:#  X: five fresh devices -- the first-refusal cells (previous selector x width) no other session's first refusal holds.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:553:#  Each: PORT=k, its FIRST refusal (tag x), then a PORT read into a poisoned register.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:558:#  B: every single-bit value from 2^3 up that each width can carry, refused after PORT=5.  One register per width
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:559:#  starts at 8 and doubles after each store (dsll); PORT is read once per width, after all of that width's refusals
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:560:#  -- a refusal that let a value through would store it as an int (2^k, INT_MIN or 0, never 5), and the later
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:561:#  refusals of that width would keep it.  The count is sampled after the last of the 108 refusals.
HEAD:regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:573:#  the tag goes on the LAST refusal (2^63 by sd), so the count is read after all of them
