1763:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the odd-step truthfulness row is present" \
1764:               "$(grep -c 'odd chunk: received never exceeds len' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1765:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the mid-walk length row is present" \
1766:               "$(grep -c 'mid-walk: one 4-byte read leaves 33' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1767:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the mid-walk cursor row is present" \
1768:               "$(grep -c 'mid-walk: cursor advances by 4' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1769:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the width length row is present" \
1770:               "$(grep -c 'width: a 2-byte read of 1 remaining' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1771:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the width cursor row is present" \
1772:               "$(grep -c 'width: received stops at data_in_len' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1773:     #  #475, second wave: four rows added after a MEASURING seat showed the detector
1774:     #  was below the vacuity floor.  Each closes something the others cannot see:
1775:     #    chunk edge  -- `Md5_xferbound` clamps to the TRANSFER remainder instead of the
1776:     #                  CHUNK remainder.  It is identical on every single-chunk transfer,
1777:     #                  so it swept all six earlier rows; it diverges only at a boundary.
1778:     #                  It also showed "odd chunk" was the wrong FRAME -- an EVEN 32768
1779:     #                  chunk overshoots too, under mixed access widths.
1780:     #    guest byte  -- the ONLY guest-visible channel here.  CYL_LO/CYL_HI are served
1781:     #                  from atapi_len, so a negative count reaches the guest as 0xffff.
1782:     #                  Without it, a fix that repairs completion but leaves the counter
1783:     #                  negative looks correct.
1784:     #    zero-length -- READ TOC with allocation length 0 arms DATAIN with a ZERO chunk.
1785:     #                  A clamp guarded `> 0` rather than `>=` hangs here.
1786:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the chunk-edge re-arm row is present" \
1787:               "$(grep -c 'chunk edge: the re-arm still fires' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1788:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the chunk-edge anchor row is present" \
1789:               "$(grep -c 'chunk edge: chunk 2 starts at 32768' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1790:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the GUEST-VISIBLE byte-count row is present" \
1791:               "$(grep -c 'guest byte count is not 0xffff' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1792:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the zero-length DATAIN row is present" \
1793:               "$(grep -c 'zero-length DATAIN stays at zero' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1794:     #  #476: the host-kill row.  It FORKS, because the defect is a SIGNAL -- an
1795:     #  in-process oracle would take the differential down with it and report nothing.
1796:     #  #475 clamped a counter that used to go NEGATIVE; that woke a branch whose
1797:     #  `d->atapi_st->data_in_len` deref had only ever been protected by the broken
1798:     #  value.  Two guest register accesses from a fresh device reached it.
1799:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the host-kill row is present" \
1800:               "$(grep -c 'a DATA read with no transfer survives' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1801:     #  #477: the phase rows.  Kill table 6/6; FOUR of the six die on a single
1802:     #  sole-carrier row.  Two exist only because a measuring seat named the escape:
1803:     #    `!= PHASE_DATAIN`  passes every DATAIN row -> the DATAOUT and COMPLETED rows
1804:     #    `& WDCI_CMD`       CMDOUT and COMPLETED both carry that bit, and the seat could
1805:     #                       NOT kill it -> the COMPLETED row is its sole carrier
1806:     #  The NO-PUSH row is the one that settles the mechanism: a len-3 write pushes ZERO
1807:     #  bytes and the dispatch frees the transfer anyway, so the defect was never "writes
1808:     #  push foreign bytes" -- which is why guarding the pushes measured WORSE than no fix.
1809:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the CDB re-dispatch row is present" \
1810:               "$(grep -c 'a mid-DATAIN write frees nothing' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1811:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the NO-PUSH row is present" \
1812:               "$(grep -c 'no-push write does not dispatch' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1813:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the DATAOUT phase row is present" \
1814:               "$(grep -c 'a DATAOUT write does not dispatch' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1815:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the COMPLETED phase row is present" \
1816:               "$(grep -c 'a COMPLETED write does not dispatch' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1817:     #  #473 pass 2: three rows added AFTER the round shipped, because two more
1818:     #  clause-(d) mutants escaped a table that had already closed four.  Both hid for
1819:     #  one structural reason -- every re-arm row sat where the remainder EXCEEDS 32768,
1820:     #  so `atapi_len` and the constant 32768 were the same number.  Md_emptyguard is
1821:     #  the round own safety sentence as a predicate; Md5_chunkconst relaxes the refill
1822:     #  bound to that constant and is a heap-buffer-overflow READ under ASan.
1823:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the dirty-ring row is present" \
1824:               "$(grep -c 're-arm: refills a NON-EMPTY ring too' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1825:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the short-last-chunk length row is present" \
1826:               "$(grep -c 'last chunk: length is the REMAINDER' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1827:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the refill-bound row is present" \
1828:               "$(grep -c 'last chunk: refill is BOUNDED by it' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1829:     #  #407: two rows added (slave drive, word 49) and row_selfconsistent() widened, after a
1830:     #  measure seat found three mutants that passed (this comment said "three rows added"
1831:     #  until the 2026-09-05 retrospective review; the commit message carries the same error)
1832:     #  the original ten. Each is named because each is the ONLY row that kills its
1833:     #  mutant -- verified by asserting the row NAME in the kill, not just that
1834:     #  something failed.
1835:     #    slave      <- 10 chars: drop `d->drive + ` at dev_wdc.c:179
1836:     #    word 49    <- 1 char:   advertise LBA that is #if 0'd out
1837:     #    65535 loop <- 11-bit cylinder truncation, first contradiction at c=2048,
1838:     #                  which the old c<=2000 bound missed by FORTY-EIGHT
1839:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the slave-drive row is present" \
1840:               "$(grep -c 'the SLAVE reports its own capacity' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1841:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the word-49 capability row is present" \
1842:               "$(grep -c 'no unimplemented capability claimed' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1843:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the oracle sweeps the full 16-bit range" \
1844:               "$(grep -c 'of 65535 contradict their geometry' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1845:     #  #408: two more named rows, each closing a mutant class that survived the
1846:     #  twelve. The slave row previously asserted only CAPACITY, so four mutants
1847:     #  reading drive 0's cylinders/heads/spt went unnoticed; and base_drive -- which
1848:     #  dev_wdc_init sets to 2 for the SECONDARY controller, so it is live, not
1849:     #  hypothetical -- was ungated at all three call sites.
1850:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the slave row covers geometry, not just capacity" \
1851:               "$(grep -c 'the SLAVE reports its own' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1852:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the base_drive row is present" \
1853:               "$(grep -c 'base_drive reaches the diskimage id' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1854:     #  #409: two further rows, each closing mutants that survived the thirteen.
1855:     #    identity  <- is_a_cdrom and getname still IGNORED `id`, so four mutants
1856:     #                 lived. The stub now poisons BY INVERSION: every id except
1857:     #                 this drive's is a CD-ROM. Pinning ONE specific id does not
1858:     #                 work -- a wrong id simply misses it and still reads "not a
1859:     #                 CD-ROM", which is how the first attempt at this row failed.
1860:     #    wide      <- no fixture pushed heads or spt past 255, so the `>> 8` half
1861:     #                 of words 3 and 6 was never non-zero and could be replaced by
1862:     #                 a literal 0 undetected.
1863:     #  #410: grep a string the row prints WHETHER IT PASSES OR FAILS. The #409
1864:     #  version matched 'the right id', which appears only in the ok line -- so a
1865:     #  genuinely FAILING row also reported "not present", turning one red row into
1866:     #  two and making a failure indistinguishable from a deletion. That is the
1867:     #  phantom-regression shape this harness's own vacuity taxonomy warns about.
1868:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the identity row is present" \
1869:               "$(grep -c 'the right id\|not the CD-ROM next to it' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1870:     #  #410: the ATAPI branch was DELETABLE with every row green -- #409's
1871:     #  inverted poison made the drive under test the only non-CD-ROM, so the row
1872:     #  could observe nothing but the negative answer. This row takes the other
1873:     #  branch and asserts word 0 == 0x8580.
1874:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the ATAPI-flags row is present" \
1875:               "$(grep -c 'announces itself as one' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1876:     check     "wdc IDENTIFY: the wide-geometry row is present" \
1877:               "$(grep -c 'high byte' "$WDCLOG")" "1"
1878: fi
1879: 
1880: # ---- #406: the autodev.c generator ----------------------------------------------
1881: # A prose mention of "DEVINIT" in a comment used to reach the generated C. It cost
1882: # 358 compiler errors, no binary, gate 1 red and eleven gates skipped -- from one
2240: #
2241: #  *** THE THIRD ASSERTION IS THE H, AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE ROUND. ***  A name in
2242: #  SC_COVERED is a LEDGER ENTRY; sc_unbacked greps the differential's OWN SOURCE for a real
2243: #  @@SELFCHECK@@ block, so moving a stem from EXEMPT to COVERED and writing no sentinel is
2244: #  RED.  Without it this would be another G -- a check that a name appears in a list -- which
2245: #  is precisely the defect being corrected here.
2246: SC_COVERED="timer memory_rw footbridge m8820x m8invread diskimage_sync diskimage_parse diskimage_io diskimage_geom sh4_tmu rtc_range fbpending_bound wdc_identify"
2247: #  *** COVERED IS NOT UNIFORM, AND THE MANIFEST CANNOT SEE THE DIFFERENCE -- READ THIS BEFORE
2248: #  TREATING A GREEN ROW AS "the file is vouched for". ***  Most COVERED stems route every row
2249: #  through one shared helper, so the sentinel covers the file.  No count is kept here: it used to say
2250: #  "seven of the ten", then "all but three" -- stale the first time a stem was added, and wrong for
2251: #  geom and sync, which each have two helpers.  The stems whose sentinel does NOT cover every row, or
2252: #  has multiple comparators:
2253: #    * sh4_tmu   -- 12 of 19 rows.  row1() is the only comparator in the file that takes an
2254: #                   expected value; row_three_timers/row_rtcsr_falls_through/row_interrupt/
2255: #                   row_interrupt_unie_clear/row_stopped/row_no_freeze each hardcode their own,
2256: #                   and cannot be probed; the 19th is the identity row.  (This said "12 of 18" and
2257: #                   "needs more than one probe" -- GPT-6 Astra, confirming #551.)
2258: #    * geom      -- every row but the identity row, via BOTH geom() and parse(), which are
2259: #                   two separate comparators and are probed separately for that reason.
2260: #    * sync      -- both checks() and checkn(), for the same reason.
2261: #    * wdc_identify -- 24 of 128 rows compare inside a comparator the sentinel feeds: 16 of
2262: #                   chk()'s 24 calls and all 8 of row_cmp()'s.  chk()'s other 8 are handed a 0/1
2263: #                   verdict computed at the call (derive: grep -cE '\? [01] : [01], 1\);'
2264: #                   regress/diff_wdc_identify.c), and the other
2265: #                   96 compare inline -- NOT covered.  "Covered" means the COMPARATOR is live,
2266: #                   not that a row is.  What this file's checks can see stops at what the file
2267: #                   itself writes.  CAN keep this gate green -- measured on #551's passes,
2268: #                   disclosed, not chased: any difference between a comparator's behaviour on the
2269: #                   self-check's calls and on real calls (a keyed test, feeding it its own want,
2270: #                   or its failure report kept from this gate's count check while the tallies are
2271: #                   hidden -- one edit or two, in the comparator's text or through a file-scope
2272: #                   macro); and covered rows skipped with their labels and counts printed.  (This
2273: #                   list also named a probe-agreeing test and an edit of the self-check itself:
2274: #                   both were measured RED on all three defect devices -- F_sum and
2275: #                   F_scweak_nocmpinc_reset in run551f.out -- so neither is a measured escape, and
2276: #                   the first one's blindness to a device whose differences compensate is a
2277: #                   reading -- GPT-6 Astra, confirming #551.)  (A log REWRITTEN by the process after
2278: #                   the rows ran passes the file-redirected MAIN checks, but the existing piped
2279: #                   self-mutant lane rejects it on all three tested defects, so it does NOT keep
2280: #                   this gate green -- GPT-6 Astra, confirming #551.)  Anything else -- a trailer
2281: #                   rewrite, a reset of either or both tallies anywhere, rows moved or deferred
2282: #                   with their counts forged -- is caught: a covered failure prints a line this
2283: #                   gate's `[0-9]+ failures' check reads at the moment it fails (check() compares
2284: #                   the newline-joined list of every captured number with the one string "0", so
2285: #                   no extra match passes, not even `0 failures').  The file covers 24
2286: #                   comparisons and reports all 32 comparator failures.  The gate-side close is a
2287: #                   lane whose device mutants fail one capacity row and one ring row, and the log
2288: #                   read through a pipe (filed: `wdcidlane').  #551, by owner ruling 2026-09-25
2289: #                   ("24/128 now, file the rest"); the rest is `wdcidcomparators'.
2290: #  rtc_range is in the UNIFORM group: every row goes through chk(), its single comparator.
2291: #  Named here rather than left to be re-derived, because the exception list above
2292: #  is only trustworthy if additions are classified as they arrive.
2293: #  The per-file comments carry the scope; this is a LIVENESS control, never a census, and
2294: #  "going from 0 to 1 is not false comfort; calling 1 `the detector works` is".
2295: #
2296: #  THE EXEMPTIONS, DATED AND REASONED.  A FORTNIGHT, matching the deadline the owner chose
2297: #  over the Q4 one I proposed for the selfmutant list: 2 uncovered differentials, 1311 and 766
2298: #  lines, whose every row could be inert with this gate green.  (True when written; #551 moved
2299: #  wdc_identify to COVERED, so ieee_store is the one left.)
2300: #
2301: #    ieee_store    HAS NO COMPARATOR TO SENTINEL.  No check(), no `rows`, no `failures`: each
2302: #                  section carries its own counter -- nine of them are the `== 0' terms of its
2303: #                  DIFF_PASS test (abs_bad, unexplained, missed, inrange_diff, rm_rn_bad,
2304: #                  rm_rz_bad, rm_d_bad, rm_vec_bad, interp_bad; this said six until #550) --
2305: #                  with the comparison written inline dozens of times,
2306: #                  and this gate greps one summary line per counter for 0.  There is no single
2307: #                  point to feed a mismatch to.  A probe through a comparator NO REAL ROW USES
2308: #                  would prove nothing while reading exactly like coverage, so none was
2309: #                  written -- a forced control is worse than none.  Gate 3 does cover this file
2310: #                  against TOTAL comparator death, which is this control's own failure mode,
2311: #                  but by the vacuity-prone `grep -c PASS == 0` form its SM_EXEMPT comment
2312: #                  already flags, so it is a weaker cover than it looks.
2313: #    wdc_identify  DISCHARGED by #551 -- moved to SC_COVERED with a self-check on its comparators (see
2314: #                  the COVERED list above).  This entry used to say "NOTHING TO FEED A MISMATCH TO"
2315: #                  and "This one needs the nine row functions refactored onto a shared helper
2316: #                  first".  The nine were 16 executions -- row() x 8 and eight zero-argument
2317: #                  functions -- and that census (2026-08-19) went stale when #484 added
2318: #                  chk(name, got, want) on 2026-08-27: its 24 calls are none of the nine.
2319: #  RE-DATED 2026-09-18 BY OWNER RULING, from 2026-09-02 -- a date that passed with no owner row: the
2320: #  "no exemption is past its dated deadline" row below went RED on every run from 2026-09-03, and the
2321: #  first run to OBSERVE it was 2026-09-18, the weekly battery having been disabled.  A re-date is not a
2322: #  discharge, and this list's SM twin was ruled the other way ("fix it, do not re-date", #537).
2323: #  #550, 2026-09-25: DECIDED SEPARATELY BY OWNER RULING 2026-09-25 (row `scexempt'); the comparison is a
2324: #  strict `<', so each entry is valid ON its own date and RED from the next day.
2325: #    ieee_store    RE-DATED to 2026-12-10, its reason above standing (nine counters, no comparator:
2326: #                  the larger refactor, dated after the wdc work).  RED from 2026-12-11.  Owner
2327: #                  row `scexempt': discharge, re-decide or delete before then.
2328: SC_EXEMPT="ieee_store:2026-12-10"
2329: 
2330: sc_missing=""

